From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: add a driver for the IDT P9320 wireless receiver

The P9320 is the Qi receiver in the Galaxy Z Flip3 (board revisions 05
and later; the earlier US boards carry an LSI S2MIW04 with another
register map). Its firmware runs the Qi protocol by itself. When a pad
is found, the driver does what Samsung's mfc_charger.c does: 5.5 V out,
the mode interrupt on, the 17 V clamp, a 1.6 A limit, CMA modulation,
the foreign object detection gains from the DT and the RPP scale. It
services the chip's interrupt line (clear INT_A, then CLEAR_INT, while
the line stays asserted) and reports the receiver as a wireless power
supply with its output voltage, current, die temperature and the input
current the charger may draw, stock's 800 mA for a basic pad.

The chip runs from the pad, so it is only set up once the pad detect
line rises; that line also wakes the phone. Samsung's fast wireless
(10 V) handshake is not part of this.

Signed-off-by: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 .../bindings/power/supply/idt,p9320.yaml           |   86 +++
 drivers/power/supply/Kconfig                       |   10 
 drivers/power/supply/Makefile                      |    1 
 drivers/power/supply/p9320_charger.c               |  507 ++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 604 insertions(+)


diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/supply/idt,p9320.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/supply/idt,p9320.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..249bc14
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/supply/idt,p9320.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/power/supply/idt,p9320.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: IDT P9320 Qi wireless power receiver
+
+maintainers:
+  - Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
+
+description:
+  The P9320 receives power from a Qi or PMA pad and runs the wireless power
+  protocol in its own firmware. Its VOUT feeds the wireless input of a
+  battery charger. The chip is powered from the pad and only answers on I2C
+  while the phone lies on one.
+
+allOf:
+  - $ref: power-supply.yaml#
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: idt,p9320
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  detect-gpios:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description: Pad detect (rectifier voltage good); asserted with a pad.
+
+  int-gpios:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description:
+      The chip's interrupt line, asserted until the host clears the
+      interrupt.
+
+  enable-gpios:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description: Receiver enable.
+
+  idt,fod:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint8-array
+    minItems: 12
+    maxItems: 12
+    description:
+      Foreign object detection gain and offset pairs for the six power
+      levels, written to the chip when a pad is found.
+
+  idt,rpp-scale:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+    default: 0x40
+    description: Received power packet scale coefficient.
+
+  idt,bpp-input-current-microamp:
+    default: 800000
+    description:
+      Input current the charger may draw from the receiver on a basic
+      power profile pad.
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+  - detect-gpios
+  - int-gpios
+
+unevaluatedProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+
+    i2c {
+        #address-cells = <1>;
+        #size-cells = <0>;
+
+        wireless-charger@3b {
+            compatible = "idt,p9320";
+            reg = <0x3b>;
+            detect-gpios = <&pmic_gpios 1 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+            int-gpios = <&tlmm 119 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+            enable-gpios = <&tlmm 153 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+            idt,fod = /bits/ 8 <0x96 0x3e 0x8e 0x37 0x96 0x2e
+                                0x96 0x31 0xaa 0x03 0x9c 0x42>;
+        };
+    };
